 PYTHONPATH=src python -m auditlog.search --actor alice --action login
 ```
 
-`--sort` and `--direction` select the ordering column and direction; both default
-to a timestamp descending sort. Results are unpaginated.
+Options:
 
-Clause building lives in `auditlog.filters`, so the same escaping and ordering
-rules apply to every query against the table.
+| Option | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `--actor` | Required. Exact actor match. |
+| `--action` | Optional. Exact action match; omit to match every action. |
+| `--since` | Optional. Only rows with `ts` at or after this ISO 8601 date or datetime, e.g. `2026-01-31`. |
+| `--sort` | Column to order by: `id`, `actor`, `action`, `target` or `ts` (default `ts`). |
+| `--direction` | `asc` or `desc` (default `desc`). |
+| `--limit` | Rows per page, 1-1000 (default 50). |
+| `--offset` | Rows to skip (default 0). |
+
+Ties are broken by `id`, so paging with `--limit`/`--offset` is stable.
+
+```sh
+PYTHONPATH=src python -m auditlog.search --actor alice --since 2026-01-01 --limit 20 --offset 20
+```
+
+All values are bound as SQL parameters. `auditlog.filters` builds the `ORDER BY`
+clause from an allowlist of audit columns and directions, since identifiers cannot
+be parameters; anything else is rejected.
